# `curl` and `wget` (TASK-27 9a; `make test-apps`, which turns @PORT@ and the
# @TLS...@ ports into numbers). C programs on OpenSSL, zlib and libposix's
# sockets and getrandom, run through the broker from the disk's /bin. The
# disk's /system/tls/roots is the test PKI's root (the production image's
# refusal of it is test-hand-run's); the names come from net.hosts. The
# shell gives each program a connection that reaches the host and port of the
# URL on its line, /system/tls to read, and nothing else.
! panicked
! first process ended
< Type help to begin, or help tour.
< \r\n~>
# The lease takes a few hundred milliseconds under TCG.
> sleep 3\r
< \r\n~>
# HTTP: a page, byte for byte; to a file; under the URL's name.
> curl -s http://10.0.2.2:@PORT@/\r
< Zoraya test page
< fetched over HTTP, byte for byte
< \r\n~>
> status\r
< \r\nsucceeded (exit status 0)
< \r\n~>
> curl -s -o page.txt http://10.0.2.2:@PORT@/\r
< \r\n~>
> cat page.txt\r
< Zoraya test page
< fetched over HTTP, byte for byte
< \r\n~>
> curl -sO http://10.0.2.2:@PORT@/pattern/1000000\r
< \r\n~>
> stat 1000000\r
< file size 1000000
< \r\n~>
> wget -q -O - http://10.0.2.2:@PORT@/\r
< Zoraya test page
< fetched over HTTP, byte for byte
< \r\n~>
> wget -q -O big.bin http://test.zoraya:@PORT@/chunked/300000\r
< \r\n~>
> stat big.bin\r
< file size 300000
< \r\n~>
> wget -q -O more.bin http://10.0.2.2:@PORT@/close/70000\r
< \r\n~>
> stat more.bin\r
< file size 70000
< \r\n~>
# What goes wrong is said, and the exit status is the program's own.
> curl -s http://10.0.2.2:@PORT@/status/404\r
< status 404
< \r\n~>
> curl -sf http://10.0.2.2:@PORT@/status/404\r
< \r\n~>
> status\r
< \r\nexit status 22
< \r\n~>
> curl -s http://10.0.2.2:1/\r
< \r\n~>
> status\r
< \r\nexit status 7
< \r\n~>
> wget -q -O - http://10.0.2.2:1/\r
< \r\n~>
> status\r
< \r\nexit status 4
< \r\n~>
# HTTPS against the test PKI's root: the handshake draws on getrandom (a
# program with no random connection cannot start one), the certificate is
# checked against the system's date, the roots and the name in the address.
> curl -s https://test.zoraya:@TLSVALID@/\r
< Zoraya test page
< fetched over HTTP, byte for byte
< \r\n~>
> wget -q -O - https://test.zoraya:@TLSVALID@/\r
< Zoraya test page
< fetched over HTTP, byte for byte
< \r\n~>
> curl -sO https://test.zoraya:@TLSVALID@/pattern/70000\r
< \r\n~>
> stat 70000\r
< file size 70000
< \r\n~>
> wget -q -O tls.bin https://test.zoraya:@TLSVALID@/pattern/1000000\r
< \r\n~>
> stat tls.bin\r
< file size 1000000
< \r\n~>
# Refused before any byte of the request is sent; the Makefile checks the
# server's log for the same. There is no option to turn the check off.
> curl https://expired.zoraya:@TLSEXPIRED@/\r
< certificate has expired
< \r\n~>
> status\r
< \r\nexit status 60
< \r\n~>
> wget -O - https://expired.zoraya:@TLSEXPIRED@/\r
< Issued certificate has expired.
< \r\n~>
> status\r
< \r\nexit status 5
< \r\n~>
> curl https://wrong.zoraya:@TLSWRONG@/\r
< no alternative certificate subject name matches target hostname 'wrong.zoraya'
< \r\n~>
> status\r
< \r\nexit status 60
< \r\n~>
> wget -O - https://wrong.zoraya:@TLSWRONG@/\r
< no certificate subject alternative name matches
< \r\n~>
> status\r
< \r\nexit status 5
< \r\n~>
# A plain server on an HTTPS address is no TLS server.
> curl -s https://test.zoraya:@PORT@/\r
< \r\n~>
> status\r
< \r\nexit status 35
< \r\n~>
# By number, the valid server's certificate is for no name that matches.
> curl -s https://10.0.2.2:@TLSVALID@/\r
< \r\n~>
> status\r
< \r\nexit status 60
< \r\n~>
# `-k` and `--no-check-certificate` are not options of the descriptions: the
# line is a usage mistake, the program gets no connection and cannot resolve
# or connect (no request is sent: the Makefile checks the server's log).
> curl -k https://test.zoraya:@TLSVALID@/\r
< Could not resolve
< \r\n~>
> wget --no-check-certificate -O - https://expired.zoraya:@TLSEXPIRED@/\r
< Permission denied
< \r\n~>
> status\r
< \r\nexit status 4
< \r\n~>
# What the line gives, exactly (ADR-0311): the network, /system/tls to read
# with it, and for -O the one file the URL names. A line without a URL
# gives no network and no roots.
> grants curl -O https://x.example/y\r
< curl gets (a POSIX program):
< read (current directory)\r\n  ~                    create and change anything in (option -O)\r\n  /system/tls          read (with the network)\r\n  /bin /usr /etc       read (system)
< net: x.example:443           reach (operand URL)
< \r\n~>
> grants curl --help\r
< read (current directory)\r\n  /bin /usr /etc       read (system)
< \r\n~>
> grants wget -O out.txt https://x.example/y\r
< wget gets (a POSIX program):
< read (current directory)\r\n  ~                    create and change anything in (option -O)\r\n  /system/tls          read (with the network)
< net: x.example:443           reach (operand URL)
< \r\n~>
# A name the shell cannot be sure of is refused before the program runs.
> curl -O https://x.example/\r
< would save under a name the shell cannot be sure of
< to say where it goes, write -o FILE
< \r\n~>
> curl -O https://x.example/a%2Fb\r
< would save under a name the shell cannot be sure of
< \r\n~>
# A URL from a value designates nothing (ADR-0224 item 2); a +net word lets it.
> set u http://10.0.2.2:@PORT@/\r
< \r\n~>
> curl -s $u\r
< from a value, outside the line's network grants
< \r\n~>
> +net 10.0.2.2:@PORT@ curl -s $u\r
< Zoraya test page
< \r\n~>
# A program may create only what the shell designated: curl -O makes the one
# file; wget without -O has none to write in the read-only directory.
> wget -q http://10.0.2.2:@PORT@/pattern/10\r
< \r\n~>
> status\r
< \r\nexit status 3
< \r\n~>
> stat 10\r
< not found
< \r\n~>
x power off
> poweroff\r
